Vault Authentication Methods
Lab Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

Understand the fundamentals of HashiCorp Vault authentication methods
Install and configure HashiCorp Vault on a Linux system
Set up and configure LDAP authentication backend in Vault
Create and manage LDAP authentication policies
Test authentication through Vault CLI using LDAP credentials
Troubleshoot common authentication issues in Vault
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, students should have:

Basic understanding of Linux command line operations
Familiarity with authentication concepts (users, groups, permissions)
Basic knowledge of LDAP directory services
Understanding of security principles and access control
Experience with text editors like nano or vim
Lab Environment
Al Nafi provides Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your dedicated Linux machine. The provided Linux machine is bare metal with no pre-installed tools, so you will need to install all required tools during the lab following the instructions provided.

Task 1: Environment Setup and Vault Installation
Subtask 1.1: Update System and Install Dependencies
First, update your system and install the necessary dependencies.

# Update package repositories
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

# Install required packages
sudo apt install -y wget curl unzip gpg software-properties-common

# Install OpenLDAP utilities for testing
sudo apt install -y ldap-utils
Subtask 1.2: Install HashiCorp Vault
Download and install the latest version of HashiCorp Vault.

# Add HashiCorp GPG key
wget -O- https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg

# Add HashiCorp repository
echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg] https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com $(lsb_release -cs) main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/hashicorp.list

# Update package list and install Vault
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y vault

# Verify installation
vault version
Subtask 1.3: Install and Configure OpenLDAP Server
Since we need an LDAP server for authentication testing, we'll install OpenLDAP.

# Install OpenLDAP server and utilities
sudo apt install -y slapd ldap-utils

# During installation, you'll be prompted for administrator password
# Use: admin123 (remember this password)

# Reconfigure slapd for proper setup
sudo dpkg-reconfigure slapd
During the reconfiguration, use these settings:

Omit OpenLDAP server configuration? No
DNS domain name: example.com
Organization name: Example Organization
Administrator password: admin123
Database backend: MDB
Remove database when slapd is purged? No
Move old database? Yes
Subtask 1.4: Configure LDAP Directory Structure
Create a basic LDAP directory structure with users for testing.

# Create LDAP configuration file
cat > /tmp/base.ldif << 'EOF'
dn: ou=people,dc=example,dc=com
objectClass: organizationalUnit
ou: people

dn: ou=groups,dc=example,dc=com
objectClass: organizationalUnit
ou: groups

dn: cn=developers,ou=groups,dc=example,dc=com
objectClass: groupOfNames
cn: developers
member: uid=john,ou=people,dc=example,dc=com
member: uid=jane,ou=people,dc=example,dc=com

dn: uid=john,ou=people,dc=example,dc=com
objectClass: inetOrgPerson
objectClass: posixAccount
objectClass: shadowAccount
uid: john
sn: Doe
givenName: John
cn: John Doe
displayName: John Doe
uidNumber: 1001
gidNumber: 1001
userPassword: {SSHA}password123
homeDirectory: /home/john
loginShell: /bin/bash

dn: uid=jane,ou=people,dc=example,dc=com
objectClass: inetOrgPerson
objectClass: posixAccount
objectClass: shadowAccount
uid: jane
sn: Smith
givenName: Jane
cn: Jane Smith
displayName: Jane Smith
uidNumber: 1002
gidNumber: 1002
userPassword: {SSHA}password456
homeDirectory: /home/jane
loginShell: /bin/bash
EOF

# Add entries to LDAP
ldapadd -x -D "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com" -w admin123 -f /tmp/base.ldif
Subtask 1.5: Verify LDAP Installation
Test that LDAP is working correctly.

# Search for all entries
ldapsearch -x -b "dc=example,dc=com" -D "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com" -w admin123

# Test user authentication
ldapwhoami -x -D "uid=john,ou=people,dc=example,dc=com" -w password123
Task 2: Vault Server Configuration and Initialization
Subtask 2.1: Create Vault Configuration
Create a configuration file for Vault server.

# Create Vault configuration directory
sudo mkdir -p /etc/vault.d

# Create Vault configuration file
sudo tee /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl << 'EOF'
ui = true
disable_mlock = true

storage "file" {
  path = "/opt/vault/data"
}

listener "tcp" {
  address     = "127.0.0.1:8200"
  tls_disable = 1
}

api_addr = "http://127.0.0.1:8200"
cluster_addr = "https://127.0.0.1:8201"
EOF

# Create Vault data directory
sudo mkdir -p /opt/vault/data
sudo chown vault:vault /opt/vault/data
Subtask 2.2: Start Vault Server
Start the Vault server in development mode for this lab.

# Start Vault server in background
vault server -config=/etc/vault.d/vault.hcl &

# Wait for server to start
sleep 5

# Set environment variable for Vault address
export VAULT_ADDR='http://127.0.0.1:8200'

# Add to bash profile for persistence
echo 'export VAULT_ADDR="http://127.0.0.1:8200"' >> ~/.bashrc
Subtask 2.3: Initialize and Unseal Vault
Initialize Vault and unseal it for use.

# Initialize Vault (save the output!)
vault operator init -key-shares=1 -key-threshold=1 > /tmp/vault-init.txt

# Display initialization output
cat /tmp/vault-init.txt

# Extract unseal key and root token
UNSEAL_KEY=$(grep 'Unseal Key 1:' /tmp/vault-init.txt | awk '{print $NF}')
ROOT_TOKEN=$(grep 'Initial Root Token:' /tmp/vault-init.txt | awk '{print $NF}')

# Unseal Vault
vault operator unseal $UNSEAL_KEY

# Authenticate with root token
vault auth $ROOT_TOKEN

# Verify Vault status
vault status
Task 3: Configure LDAP Authentication Backend
Subtask 3.1: Enable LDAP Authentication Method
Enable and configure the LDAP authentication method in Vault.

# Enable LDAP auth method
vault auth enable ldap

# Verify LDAP auth method is enabled
vault auth list
Subtask 3.2: Configure LDAP Authentication Settings
Configure Vault to connect to your LDAP server.

# Configure LDAP authentication
vault write auth/ldap/config \
    url="ldap://127.0.0.1:389" \
    userdn="ou=people,dc=example,dc=com" \
    userattr="uid" \
    groupdn="ou=groups,dc=example,dc=com" \
    groupfilter="(&(objectClass=groupOfNames)(member={{.UserDN}}))" \
    groupattr="cn" \
    binddn="cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com" \
    bindpass="admin123" \
    insecure_tls=true \
    starttls=false

# Verify LDAP configuration
vault read auth/ldap/config
Subtask 3.3: Create Vault Policies
Create policies that will be assigned to LDAP users and groups.

# Create a policy for developers
vault policy write developers - << 'EOF'
# Allow developers to read and write secrets under secret/dev/*
path "secret/data/dev/*" {
  capabilities = ["create", "read", "update", "delete", "list"]
}

# Allow developers to list secrets
path "secret/metadata/dev/*" {
  capabilities = ["list"]
}

# Allow developers to read their own user info
path "auth/ldap/users/{{identity.entity.aliases.auth_ldap_*.name}}" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
EOF

# Create a policy for regular users
vault policy write users - << 'EOF'
# Allow users to read secrets under secret/shared/*
path "secret/data/shared/*" {
  capabilities = ["read", "list"]
}

# Allow users to read their own user info
path "auth/ldap/users/{{identity.entity.aliases.auth_ldap_*.name}}" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
EOF

# List all policies
vault policy list
Subtask 3.4: Map LDAP Groups to Vault Policies
Configure group mappings to assign policies based on LDAP group membership.

# Map LDAP group 'developers' to Vault policy 'developers'
vault write auth/ldap/groups/developers policies=developers

# Create a mapping for individual users
vault write auth/ldap/users/john policies=users
vault write auth/ldap/users/jane policies=users

# Verify group mappings
vault read auth/ldap/groups/developers
vault read auth/ldap/users/john
vault read auth/ldap/users/jane
Task 4: Test LDAP Authentication
Subtask 4.1: Enable KV Secrets Engine
Enable the Key-Value secrets engine to test our authentication.

# Enable KV v2 secrets engine
vault secrets enable -path=secret kv-v2

# Verify secrets engine is enabled
vault secrets list
Subtask 4.2: Create Test Secrets
Create some test secrets that our users will access.

# Create secrets for developers
vault kv put secret/dev/database username=devuser password=devpass123

# Create secrets for shared access
vault kv put secret/shared/api-key key=shared-api-key-12345

# List secrets
vault kv list secret/
Subtask 4.3: Test Authentication with John (Developer)
Test authentication using John's LDAP credentials.

# Authenticate as John using LDAP
vault auth -method=ldap username=john password=password123

# Verify current token info
vault token lookup

# Test access to dev secrets (should work)
vault kv get secret/dev/database

# Test access to shared secrets (should work)
vault kv get secret/shared/api-key

# Try to create a new dev secret (should work for developers)
vault kv put secret/dev/config environment=development debug=true
Subtask 4.4: Test Authentication with Jane (Regular User)
Test authentication using Jane's LDAP credentials.

# Authenticate as Jane using LDAP
vault auth -method=ldap username=jane password=password456

# Verify current token info
vault token lookup

# Test access to shared secrets (should work)
vault kv get secret/shared/api-key

# Try to access dev secrets (should fail)
echo "Testing access to dev secrets (should fail):"
vault kv get secret/dev/database || echo "Access denied as expected"

# Try to create a dev secret (should fail)
echo "Testing creation of dev secrets (should fail):"
vault kv put secret/dev/test key=value || echo "Access denied as expected"
Subtask 4.5: Test Group-Based Authentication
Verify that group membership affects permissions.

# Re-authenticate as root to modify group mappings
vault auth $ROOT_TOKEN

# Add Jane to the developers group in LDAP
cat > /tmp/add-jane-to-dev.ldif << 'EOF'
dn: cn=developers,ou=groups,dc=example,dc=com
changetype: modify
add: member
member: uid=jane,ou=people,dc=example,dc=com
EOF

ldapmodify -x -D "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com" -w admin123 -f /tmp/add-jane-to-dev.ldif

# Now authenticate as Jane again
vault auth -method=ldap username=jane password=password456

# Test access to dev secrets (should now work)
vault kv get secret/dev/database

# Create a new dev secret (should now work)
vault kv put secret/dev/jane-test created-by=jane
Task 5: Advanced LDAP Configuration and Troubleshooting
Subtask 5.1: Configure Token TTL and Renewal
Configure token time-to-live settings for LDAP authentication.

# Re-authenticate as root
vault auth $ROOT_TOKEN

# Configure LDAP auth method with TTL settings
vault write auth/ldap/config \
    url="ldap://127.0.0.1:389" \
    userdn="ou=people,dc=example,dc=com" \
    userattr="uid" \
    groupdn="ou=groups,dc=example,dc=com" \
    groupfilter="(&(objectClass=groupOfNames)(member={{.UserDN}}))" \
    groupattr="cn" \
    binddn="cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com" \
    bindpass="admin123" \
    insecure_tls=true \
    starttls=false \
    token_ttl=1h \
    token_max_ttl=4h

# Test authentication with TTL
vault auth -method=ldap username=john password=password123

# Check token information including TTL
vault token lookup
Subtask 5.2: Monitor Authentication Logs
Enable and monitor Vault audit logs to track authentication attempts.

# Re-authenticate as root
vault auth $ROOT_TOKEN

# Enable file audit device
vault audit enable file file_path=/tmp/vault-audit.log

# Test authentication to generate logs
vault auth -method=ldap username=john password=password123

# View audit logs
echo "Recent authentication events:"
tail -n 5 /tmp/vault-audit.log | jq '.type, .auth.display_name, .auth.policies'
Subtask 5.3: Troubleshooting Common Issues
Test and resolve common LDAP authentication issues.

# Re-authenticate as root
vault auth $ROOT_TOKEN

# Test LDAP connectivity
vault write auth/ldap/config \
    url="ldap://127.0.0.1:389" \
    userdn="ou=people,dc=example,dc=com" \
    userattr="uid" \
    groupdn="ou=groups,dc=example,dc=com" \
    groupfilter="(&(objectClass=groupOfNames)(member={{.UserDN}}))" \
    groupattr="cn" \
    binddn="cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com" \
    bindpass="admin123" \
    insecure_tls=true \
    starttls=false

# Test with wrong password (should fail)
echo "Testing with wrong password (should fail):"
vault auth -method=ldap username=john password=wrongpassword || echo "Authentication failed as expected"

# Test with non-existent user (should fail)
echo "Testing with non-existent user (should fail):"
vault auth -method=ldap username=nonexistent password=password123 || echo "Authentication failed as expected"

# Check LDAP server connectivity
ldapsearch -x -H ldap://127.0.0.1:389 -b "dc=example,dc=com" -D "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com" -w admin123 "(uid=john)"
